{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>וְהָיָה בְכָל יַחֵם etc.</b> One needs to understand why Jacob would do so? Granted, what he did at first with the placement of the rods was out of necessity, for otherwise his labor would have been for naught, as we have written. But once there were in the flock streaked and speckled etc., he should not have taken the strong ones (הַמְקֻשָּׁרוֹת) etc., for in that case he would be taking more than half the profit, and this is not according to the law, for whenever no condition was made with the owner of the flock, by default the shepherd may take only a third of the profit, as we wrote in our commentary on the verse (Genesis 29:15) ״הֲכִי אָחִי אַתָּה״. And here Jacob had no right to save himself from the deceiver except regarding a matter that pertains to him by default law, yet it appears to me that he is taking more than half." "Or perhaps, the strong sheep (הַמְקֻשָּׁרוֹת) did not all give birth to streaked etc., but only for the most part, and Laban would take one portion of the strong and the entire portion of the weak (הָעֲטוּפִים), so that his share would reach two thirds. And even though Scripture says ״וְהַקְּשֻׁרִים לְיַעֲקֹב״, it means the majority of the strong; or he intended to recover for himself other thefts that Laban had committed against him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:10.710018354Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}